 "In THE MAP, the two dimensions are importance, how much of the context a thing occupies and how strongly it is marked, and meta-information, how much a piece says about itself and its situation. The two move independently.",
 :paras
 ["THE MAP describes importance inside the line, as face size, nose length, hats and scarves, and outside the line, as a statement at the opening, heavier marking, insertion into a running instance, and how many of a family are gathered and how often they come round. It is one dimension, so the dial that lengthens a nose also says how often a piece is gathered. Meta-information is what a piece says about itself, what it is, whose words, where it came from, where it sits, what to do with it, how long it applies. A piece can be at the highest importance with a bare name, or of ordinary importance and described in full."
 "In the Smile Lang document of 21 May 2026 I wrote of face size carrying attention, “Different sized smiles can bring different amounts of attention to the prompt using syntax, differently eyed smiles mean different levels of flexibility in following the instruction.”[[m:2026-05-21]] Size as importance is what THE MAP later carries in its importance dimension, while the eyes as strictness THE MAP keeps in the label and the version."
 "Importance as THE MAP keeps it grew in August 2026 into tiers and reminders, followed in [[t:conditions-and-actions]] and [[t:the-dialogue-suite]], and meta-information became the regions of a [[t:label]]. The faces whose size is the dial are the [[t:delimiter]]."],
